or a variety of lattices in [cite33†4 , cite34†6 , cite37†12 , cite38†14 , cite39†19 ]. Defant and Williams found numerous different interpretations of the size of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ when $L$ is a semidistributive lattice (or, more generally, a semidistrim lattice).\nL76: Notably, when $L$ is semidistributive, the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ is in bijection with the facets of a simplicial complex called the canonical join complex of $L$ (see [cite40†3 , cite33†4 ]). Hong [cite38†14 ] characterized and enumerated $\\mathsf{Pop}$ images on Tamari lattices. Choi and Sun [cite34†6 ] subsequently obtained analogous results for a variety of other lattices.\nL77: Barnard, Defant, and Hanson then unified several of these results by providing a simple Coxeter-theoretic description of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on an arbitrary Cambrian lattice of a finite Coxeter group. We aim to generalize Hong’s results concerning Tamari lattices in a different direction by studying $\\mathsf{Pop}$ images on ornamentation lattices.\nL86: Let $\\mathsf{C}_{n}$ denote the chain with $n$ nodes. The ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n})$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. In this special case, cite41†Theorem 1.1 tells us that the maximum size of a forward orbit of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on the $n$-th Tamari lattice is $n$, which agrees with the aforementioned result from [cite36†10 ].\nL87: \nL88: The next theorem characterizes the image of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice, thereby generalizing a result of Hong from [cite38†14 ].\nL89: ###### Theorem 1.2.\nL90: \nL91: Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$ and $\\delta\\in\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. For each $u\\in\\mathsf{T}$, let $o_{u}$ be the minimal ornament of $\\delta$ that properly contains $\\delta(u)$; if no such ornament exists, let $o_{u}=\\mathsf{T}$. Then $\\delta\\in\\mathsf{Pop}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T}))$ if and only if for every $u\\in\\mathsf{T}$ and every child $u^{\\prime}$ of $u$ in $\\delta(u)$, we have $\\Delta_{\\delta(u)}(u^{\\prime})\\neq\\Delta_{o_{u}}(u^{\\prime})$.\nL92: In cite17†Section 6 , we will prove that every ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ is in fact semdistributive. This means that $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ has a canonical join complex, the number of facets of which is $|\\mathsf{Pop}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T}))|$. This provides an alternative interpretation of cite42†Theorem 1.2 . See [cite37†12 , Corollary 9.10] for other interpretations of the size of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on a semidistributive lattice.\nL93: It is also natural to consider the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ when $k\\geq 2$. So far, this has not been explored in the literature, largely because it is often difficult to characterize this image. We will prove some necessary conditions for an ornamentation to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ (see cite43†Propositions 3.3 and cite44†5.3 ). These conditions are, in general, not sufficient.\nL94: However, we will obtain as a consequence a complete characterization of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))$, where $\\mathsf{C}_{n}$ is an $n$-element chain poset (see cite45†Theorem 5.4 ). This will allow us to deduce the following explicit generating function for the sizes of the images of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on Tamari lattices.\nL95: ###### Theorem 1.3.\nL96: \nL97: For each nonnegative integer $k$, we have\nL98: \nL99:  | $$\\sum_{n\\geq 0}|\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))|x^{n}=\\frac{1-x^{k+1}-\\sqrt{(1-x^{k+1})^{2}-4x(1-x)(1-x^{k+1})}}{2x(1-x)}.$$  |\nL100: When $k=0$, the generating function in cite46†Theorem 1.3 is that of the Catalan numbers, which we already knew since $\\mathsf{Pop}^{0}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n}))=\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{C}_{n})$ is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. When $k=1$, the generating function is that of the Motzkin numbers; this recovers one of Hong’s main results from [cite38†14 ].\nL101: ### 1.4. Outline\nL102: cite11†Section 2 discusses basic properties of the pop-stack operator on an ornamentation lattice and introduces some notation and terminology. We prove cite41†Theorem 1.1 in cite12†Section 3 after establishing a necessary condition for an ornamentation to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ (cite43†Proposition 3.3 ). We prove cite42†Theorem 1.2 in cite15†Section 4 . In cite16†Section 5 , we prove cite44†Proposition 5.3 , which yields another constraint on the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$.\nL103: We then characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on the $n$-th Tamari lattice in cite45†Theorem 5.4 , and we deduce cite46†Theorem 1.3 . Finally, cite17†Section 6 provides a short proof that ornamentation lattices are semidistributive.\nL303: ## 4. The Image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$\nL304: \nL305: Our goal in this section is to characterize the image of the pop-stack operator on the ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. This characterization, which we describe in cite66†Theorem 4.2 , is given by imposing a simple local criterion on each ornament in an ornamentation.\nL332: Now that we have characterized the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on an arbitrary ornamentation lattice, we turn to studying the images of higher iterates $\\mathsf{Pop}$. This turns out to be much more complicated. cite43†Proposition 3.3 gives a necessary condition in terms of $v$-ranks for an ornamentation to lie in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$. The $v$-ranks are fairly global, as determining the $v$-rank of a node could require checking every node in its subtree.\nL333: We will provide another necessary (but not sufficient) condition for an ornamentation to lie in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$, this time depending on features that are local to each ornament. We will then use this condition to completely characterize $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}(\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T}))$ when $\\mathsf{T}$ is a chain (so $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ is a Tamari lattice).\nL354: While cite43†Propositions 3.3 , cite66†4.2 and cite44†5.3 each impose necessary, independent conditions for an ornamentation to belong to $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$, their conjunction is still not a sufficient condition. Consider the ornamentation $\\delta$ in cite70†Figure 8 . None of these three results eliminate the possibility that $\\delta$ is in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{2}$.\nL355: Indeed, each node in $\\delta(v_{0})$ has a $v_{0}$-rank of at least $2$, the unique non-singleton ornament $\\delta(v_{0})$ is not hugged by any ornament (including $\\delta(\\omega)$), and the unique section of $\\delta(v_{0})$ in $\\delta$ has $2$ beads as necessary. However, it is straightforward to check that $\\delta$ is not in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{2}$.\nL356: Figure 8. An ornamentation demonstrating that cite43†Proposition 3.3 , cite66†Theorem 4.2 , and cite44†Proposition 5.3 are insufficient for guaranteeing that an ornamentation belongs to the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{2}$. Each node is labeled by its $v_{0}$-rank.\nL375: Note that the ornamentations $\\delta^{(0)},\\ldots,\\delta^{(k-1)}$ all have the same set of non-singleton ornaments. Also, if $\\delta^{(k-1)}(u)$ is not a singleton, then $u$ is not hugged in $\\delta^{(k-1)}$ by any other node in $\\mathsf{C}_{n}\\cup\\{\\omega\\}$. Therefore, cite66†Theorem 4.2 implies that $\\delta^{(k-1)}$ is in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$. Furthermore, it is not difficult to check using cite51†Lemma 2.4 that for each $t<k-1$, we have $\\mathsf{Pop}(\\delta^{(t+1)})=\\delta^{(t)}$.\nL376: It follows that $\\delta=\\mathsf{Pop}^{k-1}(\\delta^{(k-1)})$, so $\\delta$ is in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$. ∎\nL396: In this brief section, we prove that ornamentation lattices are semidistributive, which provides further motivation for our analysis of the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ in cite15†Section 4 . We will need the following result due to Barnard. Given a finite poset $P$ with partial order $\\leq_{P}$ and an element $x\\in P$, we extend our earlier notation by writing\nL397: \nL398:  | $$\\Delta_{P}(x)=\\{x^{\\prime}\\in P:x^{\\prime}\\leq_{P}x\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\nabla_{P}(x)=\\{x^{\\prime}\\in P:x\\leq_{P}x^{\\prime}\\}.$$  |\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9576view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn9570view2\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 9.6\"}); Total lines: 1931\nL1351: If $c$ is a Coxeter element of $W$, then $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$ is a quotient of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$. Let $L$ be an arbitrary lattice quotient $W_{\\equiv}$ on $W$. We begin this section by showing that $h$ is an upper bound for $\\max_{x\\in W_{\\equiv}}|\\mathcal{O}_{W_{\\equiv}}(x)|$ when $L$ is an arbitrary quotient of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ (cite222†Theorem 9.6 ). In the process, we also provide a formula for computing $\\mathrm{pop}_{W_{\\equiv}}$ for any lattice quotient $W_{\\equiv}$ of the weak order.\nL1404: ###### Theorem 9.6.\nL1405: \nL1406: If $W_{\\equiv}$ is a lattice quotient of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$, then\nL1407: \nL1408:  | $$\\max_{x\\in W_{\\equiv}}\\left\\lvert\\mathcal{O}_{W_{\\equiv}}(x)\\right\\rvert\\leq h.$$  |\nL1433: ###### Remark 9.7.\nL1434: \nL1435: One cannot weaken the hypothesis in cite222†Theorem 9.6 by assuming that $L$ is just a meet-semilattice quotient or a join-semilattice quotient. For example, suppose $W=A_{2}$. Let $\\equiv$ be the meet-semilattice congruence on $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$ with one nontrivial equivalence class consisting of $\\hat{0}$, $s_{1}$, and $s_{1}s_{2}$. The lattice $L=W/\\equiv$ is a $4$-element chain, so $\\max_{x\\in L}|\\mathcal{O}_{L}(x)|=4>3=h$.\nL1437: Our goal is now to show that the upper bound in cite222†Theorem 9.6 is tight when $L$ is a Cambrian lattice $\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$. We will do this by constructing an explicit element $\\mathbf{z}_{c}\\in\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}$ whose forward orbit under $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$ has size exactly $h$. Our construction makes use of the combinatorial AR quivers of [cite227†STW , Section 9].\nL1575:  | $$\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(\\mathbf{z}_{c})=\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}(v_{h-1})=\\{v_{h-1},v_{h-2},\\ldots,v_{0}\\}.\\qed$$  |\nL1576: \nL1577: Combining cite222†Theorem 9.6 with cite239†Lemma 9.16 yields our last main result.\nL1578: ###### Theorem 9.17.\nL1579: \nL1580: Let $W$ be a finite irreducible Coxeter group, and let $c\\in W$ be a Coxeter element. The maximum size of a forward orbit under $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\mathrm{Camb}_{c}}$ is exactly the Coxeter number $h$ of $W$.\nL1593: Now let $\\mathsf{Q}$ be a word over $S$. In [cite227†STW , Section 9.2], the Hasse diagram of $\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{Q})$ is referred to as the combinatorial AR quiver of $\\mathsf{Q}$. To explain this, consider the special case when $\\mathsf{Q}=\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ})$. By [cite227†STW , Theorem 9.3.1], there is a bijection $\\Xi\\colon\\mathrm{Heap}(\\mathsf{sort}_{\\mathsf{c}}(w_{\\circ}))\\rightarrow\\brick KQ_{c}$.\nL1632: Defant [cite57†Def22b ] proved that if $W$ is a finite irreducible Coxeter group with Coxeter number $h$, then $\\max_{x\\in W}|\\mathcal{O}_{\\mathrm{Weak}(W)}(x)|=h$. In cite222†Theorem 9.6 , we found that $\\max_{x\\in L}|\\mathcal{O}_{L}(x)|\\leq h$ whenever $L$ is a lattice quotient of $\\mathrm{Weak}(W)$, and we saw in cite223†Theorem 9.17 that this inequality is an equality whenever $L$ is a Cambrian lattice. We are naturally led to ask the following questions.\n"
